  MIXED: suppression past 3 SE on 1 of 4 gains. A result that holds on
  some gains and not others is a gain-dependent effect or a power
  problem, and this run cannot tell which. Not to be reported as either.

  THEY DO NOT COMPOSE AT ANY DRIVE IN THE WINDOW EITHER. The best lesson
  over the sweep is +0.1243 at drive 1.40 against a bar of 0.1099, with gain
  held at 0.45. The gain axis was refused first and named this axis as its
  overturning condition; both are now swept, so the negative covers BOTH
  dimensions of the oscillating window rather than one. That makes it a
  statement about the architecture: a scalar reward writing into a
  rate-weighted centroid that a mutual-inhibition attractor is already
  holding at one of its two ends.

  WHAT WOULD STILL OVERTURN IT: a cell outside the two swept ranges but
  inside the oscillating window -- the drives here span 1.05 to 2.80 at ONE
  gain, so the corners of the window are untested -- or the unsampled gain
  interval 0.45-0.60, where interpolation puts the crossing within 1% of
  the bar and is therefore not a prediction.
